{#
  The email row is written as HTML numeric character references, not as text.
  The address is real, and the encoding is deliberate at two levels:

    - in the OUTPUT, so a harvester that regex-scans the page for
      `something@something.tld` finds nothing to match;
    - in this SOURCE, so the same regex finds nothing in the public repository
      either. Encoding only the output would leave the address in plain sight
      one click away on GitHub, which defeats the point.

  A browser decodes the references before it builds the DOM, so the link works,
  copy-and-paste works, and a screen reader reads the address normally. This
  stops a naive scanner, not a scraper that parses HTML properly. Treat it as
  raising the cost, not as a guarantee.

  To read or change it, decode and re-encode with the entities, never by typing
  the address in plain form:

    python3 -c 'import html,sys; print(html.unescape(sys.argv[1]))' '<blob>'
    python3 -c 'import sys; print("".join("&#%d;"%ord(c) for c in sys.argv[1]))' '<addr>'

  `check-build.sh` decodes the built href and asserts it is a valid mailto. It
  never writes the address down, so this comment and that assertion stay true
  when the address changes.
#}
